<p>If your hair is falling more than usual, a short set of blood tests is worth doing: a full blood count with ferritin, thyroid function, vitamin B12 and vitamin D, with hormone tests added for women whose history points that way. These look for causes that are common, measurable and correctable. They do not diagnose pattern hair loss, and no supplement is a substitute for them.</p>

<p>The second half of that answer matters as much as the first. Blood tests can find a reason your hair is shedding. They cannot tell you whether the hair on your crown is miniaturising, and a normal set of results does not mean nothing is wrong. Supplements, meanwhile, correct deficiencies you actually have and do very little for the ones you do not. What follows sets out which tests earn their place, how to read them without over-reading them, and why biotin in particular causes more confusion than it solves.</p>

<h2 id="why-test-at-all">Why test at all, when hair fall is so common</h2>

<p>Hair fall is a symptom, not a diagnosis. Losing somewhere between 50 and 100 hairs a day is normal, according to the <a href="https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/hair-loss/" rel="noopener nofollow" target="_blank">NHS overview of hair loss</a>, and most people who notice more than that have one of a handful of explanations. Some are internal and show up in the blood: iron deficiency, an underactive or overactive thyroid, low vitamin B12. Some are events, such as an illness, surgery, childbirth or a crash diet, that push many follicles into their resting phase at once. And some, like genetic pattern loss, leave the blood entirely normal.</p>

<p>Testing earns its place because the first group is correctable, and because treating the wrong problem wastes months. Someone with iron deficiency who starts a course of scalp injections is paying to work around a cause that a routine blood test would have found. That is why <a href="/hair-fall-treatment-in-gurgaon">a doctor-led hair fall assessment</a> puts investigation before any treatment plan, not after it.</p>

<h2 id="the-tests-worth-doing">The blood tests worth doing</h2>

<p>There is no single "hair panel", and packages sold under that name often include tests that add cost without adding information. The useful core is short. Every test below is routine and available from any accredited laboratory, in Gurugram or anywhere else, and most do not require fasting, though the laboratory's own instructions take precedence.</p>

<h3>Full blood count and ferritin</h3>

<p>A full blood count, often listed as a CBC or haemogram, shows whether you are anaemic. Ferritin reflects your iron stores, and it is the more useful of the two for hair, because stores can run low well before haemoglobin falls. Iron deficiency is one of the most frequently identified contributors to diffuse shedding, particularly in women who menstruate, in anyone who has recently been pregnant, and in people whose diet is low in iron. Ferritin can also read higher than the true iron stores during inflammation or infection, so it is interpreted alongside the rest of the picture rather than on its own.</p>

<h3>Thyroid function</h3>

<p>TSH, sometimes with free T4, screens for both an underactive and an overactive thyroid. Either can cause diffuse thinning, along with changes in energy, weight, periods and tolerance of heat or cold. One practical point is worth knowing beforehand: hair lags behind the blood. It can take months after thyroid levels are brought under control for shedding to settle, so a corrected result does not mean the hair should look better the following week.</p>

<h3>Vitamin B12</h3>

<p>Low B12 is more likely on a vegetarian diet, which is common in India, and with long-term use of some medicines, including metformin and acid-suppressing tablets. Its link to hair fall is less direct than iron's, but a deficiency matters for the nerves and the blood in its own right, and it is simple to check. Folate is sometimes tested alongside it.</p>

<h3>Vitamin D</h3>

<p>Vitamin D deficiency is common in urban India despite the sunshine, something often attributed to indoor working hours, covered skin and air pollution. Low levels have been reported in people with several kinds of hair loss, but an association is not the same as a cause, and there is no good evidence that correcting vitamin D regrows hair by itself. It is still worth measuring, because a deficiency deserves treatment for bone and general health, and because it removes one variable when the hair is reassessed later.</p>

<h3>Hormone tests, when the history points that way</h3>

<p>For women with irregular periods, acne, increased facial or body hair, or thinning in a pattern, tests for androgens and related hormones help identify conditions such as polycystic ovary syndrome. These are ordered because of symptoms, not as a routine add-on, and some are timed to the menstrual cycle, so the doctor requesting them will say when to give the sample. <a href="/blog/female-pattern-hair-loss-in-women">How female pattern hair loss is assessed</a> explains where they fit, and <a href="/womens-hair-loss-treatment-in-gurgaon">women's hair loss treatment</a> starts from that broader workup. Men with typical pattern loss do not usually need hormone tests at all, because the process is driven by how follicles respond to normal hormone levels rather than by abnormal ones.</p>

<h3>Tests that depend on the examination</h3>

<p>Zinc, inflammatory markers, autoimmune screens and tests for infection are sometimes appropriate, but only when the history or the scalp examination gives a reason. Adding them for everyone produces borderline results that invite unnecessary treatment. The <a href="https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430848/" rel="noopener nofollow" target="_blank">StatPearls clinical review of telogen effluvium</a> frames vitamin D and zinc testing the same way: worth doing where an imbalance is suspected.</p>

<h2 id="why-a-normal-result-is-not-the-end">Why a normal result is not always the end of the conversation</h2>

<p>A laboratory reference range describes what is common in the population the laboratory measured, not what is ideal for hair. Ferritin is the clearest example. A result just above the lower cut-off is printed as normal, yet some dermatologists interpret a low-normal ferritin differently in a patient who is shedding, and there is genuine disagreement about where that line should sit. That is a reason to have results read by a doctor who has examined you. It is not a reason to chase a number with high-dose iron on your own.</p>

<p>Timing matters too. Shedding from a trigger usually becomes noticeable two to four months after the event, according to <a href="https://dermnetnz.org/topics/telogen-effluvium" rel="noopener nofollow" target="_blank">DermNet's summary of telogen effluvium</a>. By the time you test, a short illness or a spell of poor eating may be over, leaving normal blood and a shed that is still working itself out. Normal results in that situation are informative: they point towards reactive shedding that tends to settle, rather than an ongoing deficiency. Separating that from progressive thinning is the subject of <a href="/blog/telogen-effluvium-vs-pattern-hair-loss">telogen effluvium versus pattern hair loss</a>.</p>

<p>The reverse happens as well. A low vitamin D result in someone whose crown has been thinning steadily for five years is a finding, not an explanation. Correcting it is sensible. Expecting that to reverse genetic pattern loss is not.</p>

<h2 id="biotin-can-skew-your-results">Biotin: usually unnecessary, and it can skew your results</h2>

<p>Biotin is the ingredient most hair supplements lead with, and the evidence behind it is thin. True biotin deficiency is rare, because biotin is present in many foods and is also made by gut bacteria. The <a href="https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK547751/" rel="noopener nofollow" target="_blank">StatPearls review of biotin deficiency</a> notes that limited clinical evidence supports supplements in people without a documented deficiency.</p>

<p>The more practical problem is laboratory interference. Many immunoassays, the method behind a large number of hormone and marker tests, use biotin as part of their chemistry, and high-dose supplements can distort the result. Thyroid function tests are among those affected. A person taking a hair gummy can end up with thyroid numbers that look abnormal when the thyroid is fine, or reassuring when it is not. Cardiac tests such as troponin can also be affected, which matters far beyond hair.</p>

<p>The fix is simple. Tell the doctor and the laboratory about every supplement you take, including multivitamins and "hair, skin and nails" products, which often contain biotin at doses well above daily requirements. You may be asked to pause it for a few days before the sample is drawn. A prescribed medicine should never be stopped without advice, but a biotin gummy can wait.</p>

