<h2 id="what-supplements-cannot-do">What supplements cannot do</h2>

<p>Supplements have one legitimate job in hair fall: correcting a deficiency that has been shown to exist. Outside that job, their limits are worth stating plainly.</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>They cannot reverse pattern hair loss.</strong> Androgenetic thinning is driven by genetics and by how the follicle responds to hormones. No vitamin changes that.</li>
  <li><strong>They do not speed recovery from a reactive shed</strong> when nothing was deficient. Once a trigger has passed, the hair cycle resets on its own timetable, and adding capsules does not shorten it.</li>
  <li><strong>They do not treat a thyroid disorder.</strong> Hair that is falling because of an underactive or overactive thyroid improves when the thyroid is managed, not when biotin is added.</li>
  <li><strong>More is not better.</strong> Excess vitamin A and excess selenium are themselves recognised causes of hair loss, and iron taken without a deficiency can cause harm. Stacking several hair products often means doubling the same ingredients.</li>
  <li><strong>They cannot replace a diagnosis.</strong> Taking something "just in case" also muddies later testing, because a deficiency that was partly corrected before anyone measured it is harder to interpret.</li>
</ul>

<p>None of this makes supplements useless. Iron for proven iron deficiency, B12 for proven B12 deficiency and vitamin D for a documented low level are appropriate, and where a deficiency is what triggered the shedding, correcting it is the treatment. The distinction is between treating a result and treating a hope.</p>

<h2 id="when-blood-tests-will-not-explain-it">When blood tests will not explain your hair fall</h2>

<p>Several common causes of hair loss leave the blood entirely normal, and testing more widely will not find them.</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Pattern hair loss</strong> in men and women. It is diagnosed by examining the scalp, usually with trichoscopy, which shows miniaturised hairs of varying calibre. In men it tends to follow <a href="/blog/norwood-scale-explained">the stages of the Norwood scale</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>Alopecia areata</strong>, an immune condition that usually causes patches and occasionally more diffuse thinning. It is recognised on examination, and <a href="/alopecia-areata-treatment-in-gurgaon">alopecia areata treatment</a> follows a different pathway entirely.</li>
  <li><strong>Scalp conditions</strong> such as seborrhoeic dermatitis, which bring itching and flaking and sometimes increased shedding, and are treated at the scalp. <a href="/dandruff-treatment-in-gurgaon">Dandruff and scalp treatment</a> covers that side.</li>
  <li><strong>Breakage rather than loss</strong>, from tight styling, heat, bleaching or chemical straightening, where hairs snap along the shaft instead of shedding from the root. Water quality comes up constantly in Gurgaon; <a href="/blog/hard-water-pollution-and-hair-fall-in-gurgaon">what hard water and pollution really do to hair</a> separates shaft damage from true hair loss.</li>
</ul>

<p>This is why a blood test ordered on its own is half an assessment. The other half is someone examining your scalp.</p>

<h2 id="before-you-get-tested">Practical points before you get tested</h2>

<ol>
  <li><strong>Do not start supplements first.</strong> Test, then treat what the results show.</li>
  <li><strong>List everything you take,</strong> including biotin, multivitamins, protein powders and herbal or ayurvedic preparations, with doses.</li>
  <li><strong>Bring previous reports.</strong> A ferritin result from last year is useful context for this year's.</li>
  <li><strong>Write down the timeline.</strong> When did the shedding start, and what happened two to four months before it: a fever, surgery, a delivery, a diet, a new medicine, a difficult period at work or at home?</li>
  <li><strong>Take standardised photographs now,</strong> in the same light and from the same angles, so later change can be judged. <a href="/blog/how-to-read-before-and-after-photos">How to read before-and-after photographs</a> explains what makes a comparison fair.</li>
  <li><strong>Plan the follow-up.</strong> A corrected deficiency needs a repeat test, and the hair needs reassessing over months rather than weeks.</li>
</ol>

<p>If you are unsure whether your shedding warrants investigation, the <a href="/hair-loss-assessment">six-question hair loss self-assessment</a> is a reasonable place to start, though it is not a diagnosis. A consultation with <a href="/dr-nyra">a dermatologist who examines the scalp</a> decides which tests are actually indicated for you and reads the results in context. If testing finds nothing and the pattern points to genetic thinning, the conversation moves to treatments with evidence behind them, such as <a href="/blog/minoxidil-and-finasteride-in-india">minoxidil and finasteride, and their side effects</a>, and possibly to whether <a href="/blog/does-prp-work-for-hair-loss">PRP is worth adding</a> as an adjunct.</p>

